      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I see. So if the clause is poorly drafted and doesn't explicitly mention \"government action\" or \"epidemic,\" but instead uses broader language like \"acts of God\" or \"circumstances beyond the parties' control,\" how does that affect the tenant's leverage in a dispute? Specifically, does the ambiguity usually favor the landlord or the tenant in a Singapore court context?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the clause is vaguely drafted, relying on broad terms like \"acts of God\" or \"circumstances beyond the parties' control,\" the ambiguity itself becomes a battleground, and this is where the legal interpretation comes into play. In Singapore, courts tend to interpret contractual clauses narrowly, meaning the party seeking relief (the tenant, in this case) bears a heavier burden of proof to demonstrate that the event truly falls within the scope of the vague language.\n\nGenerally, in the absence of clear language, the ambiguity often leans slightly toward the landlord, as they are the party who drafted the contract and whose interests are usually protected by default interpretations. However, if the tenant can demonstrate that the event was truly unforeseeable and unavoidable—like a massive, unprecedented public health crisis—they have leverage, but they must argue that the *spirit* of the clause covers such events.\n\nIt's a high-stakes argument, and the tenant must frame the interruption not just as a bad business quarter, but as an external, overwhelming force that fundamentally prevented them from using the property as intended.\n\n***\n\n*This is a legal analysis, not legal advice. You absolutely need a qualified Singaporean lawyer to assess the strength of your position.*",
      "source": "target",
